course: dictionary
lesson: 11646
title: Clues from the past
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: An artefact is an object that people have made, such as a tool, a pot or a jewel. «The museum has a clay pot, an ancient artefact dug up from a field.» The word ancient tells you the pot is very old, so this artefact is a clue about the past.
explain: Historians and scientists study artefacts to learn how people lived long ago. «Scientists looked closely at the artefacts to see how people cooked their food.» Each object is like one piece of a jigsaw puzzle, and together the pieces show a picture of the past.
explain: In science and computing, the word has a different meaning. An artefact can be a false mark or mistake in a picture or result, caused by the method or the machine rather than the real thing. «The bright line on the photo was an artefact from the camera, not a real star.» Read the words around the word to see which meaning fits.
fact: An artefact is an object made by people, such as a tool, pot or jewel.
fact: Artefacts that are very old tell us how people lived, worked and made things long ago.
fact: Museums display artefacts so that visitors can see them and learn about the past.
fact: In science and computing, an artefact is a false mark or mistake caused by the method or machine, not by the real thing.
fact: The UK spelling is artefact; the US spelling is artifact.
exercise: «The museum has a clay pot, an ancient artefact dug up from a field.» What is an artefact in this sentence? | A kind of field | A type of food | A mark made by a camera | *An object made by people long ago
exercise: «The bright line on the photo was an artefact caused by the camera.» What does artefact mean here? | A fake photo of a person | *A false mark caused by the machine | A kind of camera lens | A real star in the sky
exercise: Which of these would be an artefact? | *A bronze sword made by people long ago | A cloud shaped like a dog | A wave in the sea | A pebble on the beach
test: Museums display artefacts so visitors can learn about the past. Why do museums show artefacts? | To sell them | To hide them from visitors | *To help people learn about the past | To make them into jewels
test: «The strange dot on the scan was an artefact, not a real injury.» What does artefact mean here? | A bone from a person | A kind of scan | *A false mark caused by the machine | An object made long ago
exam: Jo reads «The glitch on the screenshot was an artefact of the software.» Which meaning fits best? | A jewel from the museum | A very old object from the past | A pot used for cooking | *A false mark caused by the method or machine
